<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vikramaditya II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 733–746</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vikramaditya II was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He defeated the Pallavas and occupied their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, where he spared the temples and made gifts to them, and his queens built the Virupaksha and Mallikarjuna temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> to mark his victories.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vikramaditya II was the son of King Vijayaditya, whose reign of four decades had been long and peaceful, and he came to the Badami Chalukya throne on his father's death, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> inscriptions of Lakshmeshwar dated 13 January 735 record. As crown prince (Yuvaraja), around 730, he attacked the Pallava king Paramesvaravarman II with the help of the Western Ganga prince Ereyappa, and the Pallava had to sue for peace at great cost. When the Pallava king later attacked the Ganga king Sripurusha in 731, he was killed at the battle of Vilande, and Chalukya records give Vikramaditya the credit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His inscriptions show a wish to avenge the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, who a century earlier had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> and ended the reign of Pulakeshi II. Soon after his accession Vikramaditya took the side of Chitramaya against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a> in a Pallava civil war, and after 734 he invaded the Pallava kingdom again with Sripurusha. The 13-year-old Nandivarman fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, leaving behind his musical drum (katumukha), a samudraghosa instrument and a mace, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, gold and precious stones.</p><p>Entering the city, Vikramaditya spared its people and monuments, returned the spoils to the temples and made gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and to the poor; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> inscription on a pillar of the Kailasanatha temple records these acts. His inscriptions also claim victories over the Chera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kingdoms and a Kalabhra ruler. Early in his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> pushed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> during the Umayyad campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avanijanashraya-pulakeshin">Avanijanashraya Pulakeshin</a>, governor of the Lata branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, defeated an Umayyad army in 739.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Virupaksha Temple inscription calls him the conqueror of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> three times, the last capture being led by his son and crown prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtivarman-ii">Kirtivarman II</a>. His queens Lokadevi and Trilokadevi consecrated the Virupaksha (Lokeshwara) and Mallikarjuna (Trilokeshwara) temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a>, which stand at the centre of the UNESCO World Heritage monuments there. He allied with the Rashtrakutas by marriage, and in 735 he granted 50 nivartanas of land for the charity house of the Dhavala-Jinalaya at Lakshmeshwara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vikramaditya II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vikramaditya I expelled the Pallavas by about 655 and raided Kanchipuram in return, and <b>Vikramaditya II</b> occupied Kanchipuram around 740.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas recovered under Vikramaditya I, and around 740 <b>Vikramaditya II</b> occupied Kanchipuram, though he spared its temples and left a Kannada inscription in the Kailasanatha temple recording his gifts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
